        {
            "id": "sh06",
            "hsla": "hsla(355.43,95.36%,84.37%,0.790)",
            "description": "The Yahgan occupied the southern coast of the island of Tierra del Fuego. They are considered to be extinct. Most of the information on the Yahgan is from the nineteenth century. The Yahgan language was a language isolate with no known relationship to any other. The Yahgan lived in groups of one to three nuclear families who wandered in an area until the food supply was used up and then moved on. There were no higher level social or political groups.",
            "highest_bt": "South America",
            "bt": "Southern South America",
            "subsistence_type": "hunter-gatherers",
            "docs": 4,
            "hasSummary": true,
            "ocms": [],
            "hl": {},
            "term": "Yahgan"
        },
        {
            "id": "rv02",
            "hsla": "hsla(331.17,95.23%,87.82%,0.830)",
            "description": "The Yakut, including the Dolgan, are the farthest north Turkic people. They live in Yakutia, the Sovereign Sakha Republic of the Russian Federation formed in 1992, but they do not constitute a majority of the population of Yakutia. Traditionally, the Yakut bred horses and cattle and lived in yurts in small winter camps and in large tents in their summer camps. Trade in furs was important. Many Yakut now work in industrial and professional jobs and dominate in the intellectual elite of Yakutia. The Yakut language, closely related to Dolgan, is in the Altaic language family.",
            "highest_bt": "Asia",
            "bt": "North Asia",
            "subsistence_type": "other subsistence combinations",
            "docs": 18,
            "hasSummary": true,
